<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>false</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Deliberately NOT set here: <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>.

    It looks right for a library whose entire content is numbers, but it is an application-level
    runtime switch, not a compile-time one, so it flowed into Umbra.Shell and took WPF's ICU
    dependency out from under it. The symptom was a XamlParseException — "Cannot find non-neutral
    culture related to 'en-us'" — thrown the first time any popup or context menu was opened, which
    killed the process. It did not show up until the bar was running on a real desktop.

    Culture-independence is enforced where it actually matters instead: every parse and format that
    could be affected passes CultureInfo.InvariantCulture explicitly, and
    LuminanceTests.Hex_RoundTrips_Independently_Of_Culture guards the one round-trip that converts
    between text and colour.
  -->

</Project>
